{% trace-scenario title="You need to track LLM costs—but are you also running experiments?" prompt="Your team runs multiple LLM models in production and wants cost visibility. But as you scale, you're wondering if you also need to measure output quality and run A/B tests. Should you invest in a cost-tracking platform, or do you need evaluation capabilities too?" outcome="This guide shows you when open-source cost-tracking is sufficient and when managed evaluation justifies moving to a commercial platform." /%}

## At a glance

![Comparison of cost dashboard on LangWatch side and experiment-tracking interface on Uncoil side](/media/langwatch-vs-uncoil-hero.svg)

Your team is evaluating LLM observability tools and wondering: is an open-source cost-tracking solution like LangWatch enough, or should you adopt a commercial platform with built-in evaluation and experiment tracking?

LangWatch gives you {% highlight %}cost transparency without vendor lock-in{% /highlight %}. It's self-hosted, built for teams that own their infrastructure and want granular cost attribution down to the individual request. Uncoil adds a managed layer on top of cost tracking: {% highlight %}evaluation workflows, A/B experiment runners, and quality scoring{% /highlight %} built into the platform itself, with no separate service to deploy.

The decision hinges on whether your primary need is cost visibility alone, or whether you also need structured evaluation and experimentation to improve model outputs. It also hinges on something teams underweight early: who owns the operational burden of running the observability layer itself, and what that burden costs in engineering hours once volume climbs.

### The operating model

This article uses a decision-routing framework, expanded from a simple feature checklist into something closer to how engineering leads actually make the call — need, team capacity, deployment constraints, and cadence of iteration:

| Decision point | Question | Cost-tracking focus | Eval-driven focus |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Primary need | Are you tracking costs or improving quality? | Sufficient | Insufficient |
| Team size | How many engineers iterate on prompts? | Scales easily | Benefits from managed workflows |
| Deployment | Do you require self-hosted infrastructure? | Possible | Limited |
| Cadence | Are you running experiments weekly? | Not the primary use case | Core workflow |
| Ops capacity | Do you have someone who owns database SLAs? | Required | Not required |
| Data residency | Must LLM traces stay on-premises? | Native fit | SaaS-only |

Keep this table in mind as you read — every worked scenario below maps back to one or more of these rows.

## LangWatch: The cost-tracking foundation

LangWatch is an open-source observability platform purpose-built for cost tracking and audit. It gives you {% highlight %}real-time visibility into every LLM API call{% /highlight %}—model, provider, tokens used, cost per call, and cumulative spend by project. It's the kind of tool a platform team reaches for when the first production incident is a surprise invoice, not a quality complaint.

### What LangWatch does well

**Cost attribution at scale.** LangWatch tracks costs down to the individual request. You can see cost breakdowns by model, by endpoint, by user, or by feature. This level of granularity is essential if you're running multiple models or providers (GPT-4, Claude, Llama, and others) in the same system, because aggregate spend numbers hide which specific call site is burning budget. A team running five different agent workflows behind one API gateway needs per-workflow cost splitting, not a single top-line number — and this is exactly where LangWatch's request-level tagging earns its keep.

**Self-hosted ownership.** You deploy LangWatch in your own infrastructure—Kubernetes, Docker, or a VM. Your LLM telemetry stays inside your network. No API calls home. No SaaS dependency. For regulated industries or teams with strict data residency requirements, this is non-negotiable, and it's the single biggest reason teams choose LangWatch over any managed alternative regardless of feature parity.

**Integration simplicity.** LangWatch integrates with common LLM frameworks: LangChain, LiteLLM, the OpenAI SDK, the Anthropic SDK, and OpenTelemetry-compatible tracing pipelines. You add a few lines to instrument your code, point it at your LangWatch instance, and start seeing costs. The SDK footprint is minimal, and because it rides on OpenTelemetry conventions in many deployments, teams that already have a tracing pipeline can often route spans to LangWatch as an additional exporter rather than re-instrumenting from scratch.

**No ongoing operational cost per call.** Once deployed, LangWatch runs as a background service. There's no per-token pricing, no usage-based billing on the observability layer itself. You pay infrastructure costs (compute, storage) but not per-call licensing fees. At genuinely small volumes — a side project, an internal tool with a handful of users — this can mean LangWatch costs you almost nothing beyond a small VM.

**Dataset export for offline analysis.** Because LangWatch stores raw cost and usage records in a database you control, exporting a slice of traffic for offline analysis is a straightforward query rather than an API negotiation. Teams doing ad-hoc financial reconciliation often prefer direct SQL access over a dashboard-only view.

### The cost-tracking ceiling

LangWatch excels at answering "how much did that request cost?" But it stops there. It doesn't tell you:

- Did the output actually solve the user's problem?
- Is model A better than model B for this use case?
- Which prompt revision improved the score?
- Should we enable this feature, or roll it back?
- Is a cost-saving model swap secretly degrading answer quality?

These questions require {% highlight %}structured evaluation{% /highlight %}: running outputs through scoring functions, collecting user feedback, or comparing models side-by-side. LangWatch has no built-in eval framework. You'd need to wire up evaluation logic yourself—external scorers, human review pipelines, or custom metrics glued together with a cron job and a spreadsheet.

For teams that only need cost visibility, this is fine. But if you're iterating rapidly on models or prompts, the lack of managed evaluation becomes friction — and that friction compounds. Every week without a structured eval loop is a week of shipping prompt changes on intuition, then finding out from support tickets whether the change helped or hurt.

> **Field note:** Cost tracking is a prerequisite for production LLM systems, not a differentiator. The question is whether it's your only need.

## Uncoil: Cost tracking + managed evaluation

Uncoil bundles cost tracking with a {% highlight %}managed evaluation platform{% /highlight %}. You get all the cost visibility that LangWatch provides, plus:

**Built-in evaluation workflows.** Define scorers (LLM-based, rule-based, or custom function scorers) once, then run them across your traces automatically. Track quality metrics over time. Uncoil scores every trace in real time, and lets you backfill scores against historical traces when you add a new scorer — so you don't lose the ability to evaluate last month's traffic just because the scorer didn't exist yet.

**Experiment tracking and routing.** Run A/B tests natively. Split traffic between two prompts or models, measure quality and cost tradeoffs, and route production traffic to the winner. All managed through the platform UI, with statistical significance tracked automatically rather than hand-rolled in a notebook.

**Quality dashboards.** Track quality scores (accuracy, relevance, hallucination risk) alongside costs. See when a cost optimization accidentally degraded quality — the two numbers live on the same chart, not in two different systems you have to mentally correlate.

**Annotation queues for human review.** Not every quality question can be answered by an automated scorer. Uncoil includes queues where a human reviewer can sample traces, apply labels, and feed those labels back into scorer calibration — closing the loop between automated and human evaluation without a separate tool.

**Managed infrastructure.** Uncoil runs on Tinybird (for raw spans) and Convex (for app metadata and experiments), with Redis caching between the two layers and the dashboard. No self-hosting required. Data is encrypted in transit and at rest.

### When does evaluation matter?

Evaluation becomes critical when you move from "track costs" to "improve quality while managing costs." Common scenarios:

**Prompt iteration.** You're A/B testing different prompt versions. LangWatch shows you the cost difference. Uncoil shows you cost *and* quality difference, and automatically routes traffic to the best version once a threshold of statistical confidence is reached.

**Model selection.** You're deciding between GPT-4 and Claude for a specific task. LangWatch shows cost. Uncoil measures accuracy, latency, and hallucination risk for the same requests, then compares ROI (quality gained per dollar spent) so the decision isn't "which model is cheaper" but "which model is cheaper per unit of quality delivered."

**RAG pipeline optimization.** You're tuning retrieval depth, chunking strategy, or reranker choice. LangWatch tracks the cost of each retrieval call. Uncoil measures retrieval relevance and final answer quality, showing whether the tuning actually helped — a distinction covered in more depth in our [RAG evaluation guide](/blog/rag-evaluation-guide), which walks through the specific metrics that separate "retrieval looks fine" from "retrieval is actually helping the model answer correctly."

**Feature rollout.** You've built a new LLM-powered feature. LangWatch tracks its cost impact. Uncoil shows whether users find it useful (via structured evals) before you roll it out to 100%, catching a regression while it's affecting 5% of traffic instead of all of it.

**Guardrail regression testing.** You've added a new safety guardrail or content filter. Cost tracking can't tell you whether the guardrail is over-triggering on legitimate requests. A structured eval pass against a labeled test set can, and Uncoil runs that pass as part of the same pipeline that scores production traffic.

These workflows are possible with LangWatch, but they require building evaluation logic yourself and maintaining it as prompts evolve. Uncoil makes them native.

## Architecture and deployment model

The deployment model shapes your operational overhead significantly — arguably more than any feature comparison, because operational overhead is the cost that shows up every month whether or not you notice it.

![Diagram showing LangWatch self-hosted stack on the left (LLM app → SDK → LangWatch server → Database) and Uncoil managed stack on the right (LLM app → Uncoil SDK → Tinybird + Convex)](/media/langwatch-vs-uncoil-architecture.svg)

**LangWatch (self-hosted):**
- You run the LangWatch server, typically behind a load balancer with two or more replicas for availability.
- You manage the database (PostgreSQL or a similar relational store), including indexing strategy as trace volume grows into the millions of rows.
- Many deployments add a search layer (OpenSearch or Elasticsearch) once trace search and filtering become slow on the primary database alone.
- You handle backups, upgrades, and scaling — including capacity planning before a launch or marketing push that spikes traffic.
- Infrastructure cost: roughly $200–500/month for a small deployment, scaling with usage and with the additional search/cache layers larger deployments tend to add.
- Operational overhead: high. You own the SLA, the on-call rotation, and the postmortems when something breaks at 2 a.m.

**Uncoil (managed):**
- Uncoil runs infrastructure; there is no server for your team to patch or scale.
- Tinybird handles time-series telemetry (spans, metrics) and is built specifically for high-cardinality, high-throughput analytical queries — the same class of workload LangWatch's database layer has to be tuned for by hand.
- Convex handles app state (experiments, evals, runs, comments, alerts).
- Redis caches between Tinybird and the dashboard so repeated dashboard queries don't re-scan raw spans.
- Infrastructure cost: included in subscription; transparent per-trace pricing with no separate line item for compute, storage, or search indexing.
- Operational overhead: low. Uncoil owns the SLA, the on-call rotation, and the capacity planning.

For a small team (1–5 engineers), self-hosting LangWatch is reasonable — the infrastructure is simple enough that one person can own it as a part-time responsibility. For teams over 10 engineers, or those running high-volume production systems where the database layer has outgrown a single instance, managed infrastructure often wins decisively on total cost of ownership, because the operational burden stops scaling linearly with headcount and starts requiring dedicated expertise.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Hidden costs of self-hosting" title="Factor in more than just compute." body="Self-hosting means on-call support, database maintenance, monitoring the monitoring system, and keeping dependencies patched. For small teams, this overhead easily exceeds a managed-platform subscription. For larger teams, it often means hiring or reassigning an SRE specifically to own the observability stack — a cost that rarely appears in the initial 'LangWatch is free' calculation." tone="yellow" /%}

## Total cost of ownership: worked numbers at two scales

Rather than treating "self-hosted is cheaper" as a general claim, it's worth running the numbers at two concrete scales, because the shape of the tradeoff changes as volume grows.

![Bar chart comparing LangWatch self-hosted total cost of ownership against Uncoil managed pricing at small scale (300K requests/month) and large scale (3M requests/month), broken into infrastructure and engineering ops time](/media/langwatch-vs-uncoil-cost-comparison.svg)

**Small scale: 10,000 requests/day (~300,000/month), 3-engineer team.**

LangWatch:
- Server infrastructure: $300/month (compute, storage, networking for a single-node deployment)
- Engineer time for setup and maintenance: ~10 hours/month (~$2,000 at $200/hour fully loaded), covering patching, backup verification, and the occasional slow-query investigation
- Total: ~$2,300/month

Uncoil:
- Per-trace pricing: $0.001 × 300,000 = $300/month, transparent and predictable
- Managed infrastructure included; no separate ops line item
- Total: ~$300/month

At this scale, LangWatch's self-hosting overhead is roughly 7.7× the platform cost — almost entirely engineering time, not infrastructure. The infrastructure line items are actually comparable ($300 vs. $300); the gap is created by the ten hours per month a self-hosted deployment demands from a human.

**Large scale: 100,000 requests/day (~3,000,000/month), 12-engineer team.**

LangWatch:
- Server infrastructure: $2,500/month — a self-hosted deployment at this volume typically needs a multi-node database with read replicas, a dedicated search index for trace filtering, and enough headroom to absorb traffic spikes without falling behind on ingestion
- Engineer time: ~40 hours/month (~$8,000 at $200/hour), reflecting the jump from "patch and back up" to "own a distributed system's availability, including sharding decisions, index bloat, and query performance tuning as trace volume climbs into the tens of millions"
- Total: ~$10,500/month

Uncoil:
- Per-trace pricing: $0.001 × 3,000,000 = $3,000/month
- Managed ops: negligible on your side — roughly 2 hours/month (~$400) spent on configuring experiments, inviting team members, and reviewing dashboards, not infrastructure
- Total: ~$3,400/month

At large scale the ratio narrows slightly to about 3.1×, but the absolute dollar gap widens from roughly $2,000/month to roughly $7,100/month. That's the number that matters to a budget owner: self-hosting doesn't just cost more in percentage terms as you scale, it costs meaningfully more in real dollars, because the engineering-time component grows faster than the infrastructure component does.

| Scale | LangWatch advantage | Neutral zone | Uncoil advantage |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| < 5K requests/day | Self-hosting cost is lower; ops burden is manageable by one part-time owner | Tradeoff: low overhead vs. low usage cost | — |
| 5K–50K requests/day | — | Tradeoff: balanced; team capacity and evaluation needs matter most | Managed infrastructure becomes easier; eval features valuable |
| > 50K requests/day | — | — | Operational overhead dominates in absolute dollars; managed platform wins on total cost of ownership |

At small scales (under 1,000 requests/day), even a bare-bones self-hosted LangWatch deployment is close to free, and the choice comes down almost entirely to whether you need evaluation, not cost. At large scales (over 100,000 requests/day), Uncoil's per-trace pricing stays linear and predictable, while self-hosting costs — especially the engineering-time component — tend to step up in jumps every time the current architecture hits a scaling wall.

## Decision routing framework

Match your situation against the table below to find the right choice:

| Your situation | LangWatch fits | Uncoil fits | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cost tracking only; no evaluation | ✓ | — | LangWatch solves the stated need; Uncoil adds unnecessary complexity |
| Cost tracking + manual evaluation | ✓ | ✓ | Both work; LangWatch requires more custom code for eval workflows |
| Cost tracking + weekly prompt experiments | — | ✓ | Uncoil's experiment runner saves weeks of engineering |
| Strict data residency (on-prem required) | ✓ | — | LangWatch is self-hosted; Uncoil is SaaS only |
| < 5K requests/day; small team | ✓ | — | Infrastructure cost and ops burden favor self-hosting |
| > 50K requests/day; growth team | — | ✓ | Operational overhead tips toward managed platform in absolute dollars |
| Quality scoring must be built-in | — | ✓ | Uncoil integrates evaluation; LangWatch doesn't |
| No dedicated ops/SRE capacity | — | ✓ | Managed SLA removes the on-call burden entirely |
| Need SQL-level access to raw records | ✓ | — | Self-hosted database gives direct query access |
| Need statistical A/B routing in production | — | ✓ | Uncoil automates significance testing and traffic routing |

## Evaluation at scale: When it becomes critical

As your LLM deployment matures, several scenarios make structured evaluation non-negotiable.

**Model consolidation.** You're running Claude, GPT-4, and Llama models for different tasks. Which performs best on your specific prompts? Cost tracking tells you spend per model. Evaluation tells you quality per model. Only together can you make ROI decisions. This is where evaluation-driven observability becomes essential, as explained in our guide to [LLM observability metrics that matter](/blog/llm-observability-metrics-that-matter) — cost is one metric among several, and treating it as the only one leads to decisions that look good on a spend dashboard and bad in a support queue.

**Prompt versioning.** Your team iterates on system prompts weekly. LangWatch shows cost impact. Uncoil's built-in evaluation framework shows quality impact. You can measure whether a prompt change improved accuracy, reduced hallucinations, or improved latency—all in the same dashboard where you track cost, so a prompt-review meeting doesn't require stitching together two separate exports.

**Feature flag rollouts.** You've built an LLM-powered feature for customer support. Before rolling out to 100%, you want to verify it actually helps (via structured eval) and measure the cost impact. LangWatch alone requires you to wire this up yourself, typically with a homegrown scoring script and a spreadsheet tracking rollout percentages by hand. Uncoil has experiment workflows built in, with the rollout percentage and the quality/cost comparison living in the same view.

**Compliance and auditing.** Regulated industries (healthcare, finance) need to prove that LLM outputs meet quality thresholds. A dashboard showing "we track costs" isn't enough. You need "we measure quality, and every deployment is scored against defined criteria, with a retained audit trail of when scores changed and why." This moves beyond observability into operational governance, where managed eval platforms excel because the scoring pipeline and the audit trail are the same system.

**Regression detection after a model or provider upgrade.** LLM providers update models underneath the same API name more often than most teams expect. A prompt that scored well last month can silently degrade this month with no code change on your side. Cost tracking won't catch this — spend might look identical. A continuous eval pipeline scoring live traffic against a stable rubric is the only reliable way to notice a provider-side regression before customers do.

## Practical comparison: A worked scenario

Let's walk through a realistic scenario: a startup building an AI-powered research assistant that summarizes documents and answers questions about them.

**Week 1: Launch with cost tracking only**

The team deploys LangWatch to track costs as they test different Claude and GPT-4 models. Initial spend is $200/day. Cost tracking shows they're using Claude for reasoning and GPT-4 for quick summaries. Infrastructure for LangWatch: $150/month, run on a single small VM.

LangWatch solves the question: "how much are we spending?"

**Week 4: First user complaints**

Early users report that summaries sometimes miss key details or include hallucinations. The team starts collecting user feedback manually, pasting complaints into a shared doc. They want to know:
- Which model produces fewer hallucinations?
- Are longer documents handled worse than short ones?
- Do specific prompt versions improve accuracy?

LangWatch shows cost breakdown by model, but not quality breakdown. The team realizes they need evaluation, and now they face a choice.

**Option A: Build evaluation on LangWatch**

The team writes an eval script: feed a sample of traces through an LLM-based scorer that checks for hallucinations. They run it weekly, store results in a separate database, and build charts in Grafana to visualize scores next to the LangWatch cost dashboard. This works, but costs roughly 2 engineer-days per sprint just to maintain the eval pipeline — updating the scorer prompt, re-running against edge cases, and debugging why the scoring job silently stopped for three days. Cost of ownership: roughly $1,000/week in engineering time, on top of the LangWatch infrastructure cost.

**Option B: Migrate to Uncoil**

Same week, they enable Uncoil's managed evaluation. Define a scorer (an LLM-based hallucination detector), enable it for all new runs. Within an hour, they see quality scores alongside costs. They run an experiment: Claude vs. GPT-4 on the same 100 documents. Results show Claude is 15% more accurate and 20% cheaper for this task. This single finding — reached in an afternoon rather than a sprint — justifies the platform subscription on its own.

The difference: Option A took weeks of custom engineering and ongoing maintenance to reach an answer. Option B took one hour to set up and gave an actionable insight within the same day.

**Week 12: A second worked scenario — the cost-cutting trap**

Three months in, the team notices GPT-4 costs climbing as document volume grows, and a well-meaning engineer proposes swapping to a cheaper model for the summarization step to cut spend by 40%.

On LangWatch alone, the team ships the swap, watches costs drop as predicted, and declares victory — until, two weeks later, support tickets about missed details in summaries triple. Nothing in the cost dashboard flagged the regression, because cost dashboards only measure cost. The team has to reconstruct what changed and rebuild trust with affected customers.

On Uncoil, the same swap triggers an automatic quality comparison as part of the experiment workflow: the cheaper model shows a 40% cost reduction but also an 18-point drop in the accuracy scorer on the same document set. The team sees this before the swap reaches 100% of traffic, keeps the cheaper model for short documents where quality holds, and reverts it for long documents where it doesn't — capturing most of the savings without the regression. This is the paired cost-and-quality decision covered in [How to Reduce LLM Costs Without Hurting Quality](/blog/reduce-llm-costs-without-hurting-quality): cost cuts that aren't validated against a quality signal are gambles, not optimizations.

## SDK integration and developer experience

The practical switching cost depends on how easily each tool integrates with your stack.

**LangWatch SDK:**
```typescript
// Minimal instrumentation
import { LangWatch } from "langwatch/sdk";

const client = new LangWatch({
  apiKey: process.env.LANGWATCH_API_KEY,
  endpoint: "https://your-langwatch-instance.internal"
});

// Log a single request
await client.logRequest({
  model: "claude-3-opus",
  tokens: { input: 150, output: 200 },
  cost: 0.03,
  timestamp: Date.now(),
});
```

**Uncoil SDK:**
```typescript
// Similar signature, adds eval integration
import { Uncoil } from "uncoil/sdk";

const client = new Uncoil({
  apiKey: process.env.UNCOIL_API_KEY,
  projectId: "my-project"
});

// Same logging, plus eval context
await client.logTrace({
  model: "claude-3-opus",
  input: userQuery,
  output: response,
  metadata: { userId, conversationId },
  eval: {
    scorer: "hallucination_detector",
    context: userDocs
  }
});
```

Both clients follow the same basic shape: instantiate once, log per request. The meaningful difference is what happens after the call returns. LangWatch's `logRequest` writes an immutable cost record and nothing more. Uncoil's `logTrace` writes the same cost data but also enqueues the trace for whatever scorers are configured on the project — no additional call, no separate pipeline to stand up.

For teams already on LangWatch, the SDK migration is mechanical—swap the import, update config, done. Most teams report the code change itself takes under an hour; the remaining migration time (covered below) goes to verification, not rewriting instrumentation. For new projects, the decision is purely feature-based: do you need evaluation now or later?

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Developer experience note" title="Both SDKs are low-friction; the difference is in post-logging workflows." body="LangWatch logs are immutable cost records. Uncoil logs become input for evaluation and experiments. If your team runs experiments weekly or needs real-time quality scoring, Uncoil's integration pays off fast. If cost tracking and occasional manual review is sufficient, LangWatch's simplicity is an advantage." tone="dark" /%}

## Making the transition

If you start with LangWatch and later decide you need evaluation capabilities, the migration path is straightforward:

1. **Keep your cost data.** LangWatch stores historical costs in your own database. Export or query this history for audit or reconciliation rather than deleting it.
2. **Instrument for Uncoil.** Swap the LangWatch SDK for Uncoil's SDK (same signature in most cases) — typically a single import and one or two configuration lines, not a rewrite of your call sites.
3. **Run both in parallel.** For a week, send traces to both systems and verify costs match between LangWatch and Uncoil's billing dashboard before cutting over production traffic.
4. **Retire LangWatch.** Stop the self-hosted deployment once data consistency is verified and Uncoil is stable — or keep it running at reduced capacity for a redundant compliance record.

The switchover typically takes a single engineering day, most of it spent on the parallel-run verification step rather than the code change itself. There's no lock-in risk with LangWatch—it's designed to be replaceable, and its data lives in a database you control from day one.

Conversely, if you adopt Uncoil for evaluation but later decide you want cost-tracking redundancy (for audit or compliance), you can run LangWatch alongside Uncoil. Many teams do this for regulated industries where dual audit trails are required — one vendor-managed, one self-hosted, cross-checked periodically.

> **Decision rule:** Start with LangWatch if cost tracking alone solves your immediate problem. Migrate to Uncoil when you need evaluation workflows or when operational overhead becomes expensive.

## Integrating with your observability stack

Both tools fit into broader observability architecture, but they solve different layers.

**LangWatch in a cost-focused stack:**
- LLM SDK → LangWatch → your alerting (Datadog, Prometheus, or a custom webhook)
- Strengths: simple, self-contained, easy to audit, minimal moving parts
- Limitation: no built-in quality signal; external eval tools required, which means a second data model to maintain and reconcile against the first

**Uncoil in an end-to-end stack:**
- LLM SDK → Uncoil → quality dashboards, experiments, routing decisions, alerting
- Strengths: integrated eval, experiment platform, cost and quality in one system with one data model
- Limitation: managed service means less control over data residency and less flexibility for exotic custom storage requirements

For teams already running Datadog or New Relic, LangWatch integrates as a cost plugin sitting alongside existing infrastructure monitoring — a natural fit if your alerting culture is already built around those tools. For teams building product around LLM quality (raising the bar on RAG relevance, or reducing hallucinations as a core product commitment), Uncoil's integrated evaluation layer means fewer tools to maintain and one fewer place for cost and quality numbers to silently drift apart.

## Operational overhead in detail

Let's be concrete about the ongoing burden of self-hosting versus managed platforms, beyond the aggregate numbers above.

**LangWatch self-hosting burden (monthly, small deployment):**
- Kubernetes or VM patches: 2–4 hours
- Database maintenance, backups, recovery testing: 4–6 hours
- Monitoring the monitoring system (alert noise, disk usage, retention policy tuning): 2–3 hours
- Debugging slow queries or index bloat: ad-hoc, 2–8 hours when it happens, usually in a burst right when trace volume crosses a threshold
- Scaling decisions (CPU, memory, storage): 1–2 hours as usage grows

**Total:** 10–25 hours/month for a small deployment. At $200/hour fully loaded, that's $2,000–$5,000/month in engineering time — before infrastructure cost is even added.

**LangWatch self-hosting burden (monthly, large deployment):** multi-node database operations and failover drills, search-index cluster health and reindexing, incident response and postmortems, capacity planning ahead of traffic spikes, and security patching across a larger service surface together run **28–45 hours/month**, consistent with the $8,000/month ops figure used in the worked scenario above.

**Uncoil managed platform burden:**
- Initial setup: 1–2 hours
- Ongoing: monitoring usage, inviting team members, configuring experiment workflows, reviewing scorer results: 1–2 hours/month at small scale, closer to 2–4 hours/month at large scale as more experiments and scorers are in flight

**Total:** roughly 1–4 hours/month in steady state, regardless of scale, because the infrastructure operations don't fall on your team at all.

At 10+ requests per second (typical for a mid-size production LLM system), LangWatch self-hosting often costs $3,000–$10,500/month when infrastructure and operational time are both included. Uncoil's per-trace pricing typically falls into the $500–$3,500/month range at the same scale. The intersection point where Uncoil becomes cheaper than self-hosting is lower than most teams expect — often well before a team feels "big enough" to justify a managed platform.

{% highlight %}The hidden cost of self-hosting often exceeds the platform subscription cost at scale.{% /highlight %}

## Failure modes and recovery

Understanding what breaks is as important as understanding what works, because the failure mode tells you who is on the hook when it happens.

**LangWatch failure mode: Disk fills up**
- Symptom: cost tracking stops working; new traces aren't logged, and the gap is invisible until someone checks the dashboard
- Root cause: database grows without pruning; disk is full
- Recovery: restore from backup (assumes backups were working and were tested), prune old data, scale storage
- Time to recovery: 30 minutes to several hours, depending on backup freshness and who's paged
- Lesson: you own the SLA, including the parts of the SLA you didn't know you needed until this happened

**Uncoil failure mode: Eval scorer crashes**
- Symptom: quality scores stop flowing; cost tracking continues uninterrupted because the two pipelines are decoupled
- Root cause: eval server error or a misconfigured scorer definition
- Recovery: Uncoil investigates and fixes; you get notified through the platform's status channel
- Time to recovery: 5–30 minutes, SLA-backed
- Lesson: Uncoil owns the SLA, and a failure in one subsystem (evaluation) doesn't take down the other (cost tracking)

For {% highlight %}critical production systems where downtime is expensive{% /highlight %}, SLA ownership matters more than almost any feature comparison. LangWatch asks: "can your team commit to owning observability availability, including the 2 a.m. pages?" Uncoil asks: "can we charge you for managed availability, so that question never lands on your team?"

## Real-world assessment: Questions to ask your team

Before making a decision, have these conversations with your team:

1. **Cost control:** "Are cost alerts and monthly spend reports enough, or do we need real-time visibility by feature, user, or model?" If the latter, both tools work; if cost is the only concern, LangWatch suffices.

2. **Evaluation pace:** "How often do we run experiments? Monthly? Weekly? Daily?" Weekly-or-more makes Uncoil's experiment runner valuable almost immediately. Monthly or less, LangWatch plus manual eval is fine, at least for now.

3. **Operational readiness:** "Do we have someone on-call for database outages and scaling decisions, and are they willing to keep doing that as volume grows 10x?" If yes, self-hosting is manageable. If no, or if that person is also doing three other jobs, managed platforms reduce risk meaningfully.

4. **Team growth trajectory:** "Will our team double in the next year, and will our trace volume grow faster than our team?" High growth makes Uncoil's simplicity increasingly valuable, because operational burden that scales with trace volume rather than headcount is the kind that quietly overwhelms a team. Static teams can handle LangWatch's operational load indefinitely.

5. **Data governance:** "Can our LLM traces go to a third-party SaaS, or must they stay on-premises?" If on-premises is required by policy or contract, LangWatch is the only option among the two — this question alone can end the debate.

Answer these five questions honestly, ideally in a shared document rather than a hallway conversation. Your answers will likely point clearly toward one tool or the other, and having them written down makes the decision easy to revisit in six months.

## When to stay with LangWatch long-term

Not every team should migrate. LangWatch remains the right choice if:

1. **Your evaluation is lightweight.** You measure quality via user satisfaction surveys, not automated scoring. A monthly report is sufficient, and the overhead of a real-time eval pipeline would outweigh the benefit.
2. **You have DevOps expertise in-house already.** Your team runs Kubernetes, PostgreSQL, and monitoring stacks for other services already. Operational overhead for LangWatch is incremental, not a new skill set the team has to acquire.
3. **Compliance requires on-prem.** Data residency is non-negotiable, whether by regulation or by contract. You can't use a SaaS platform for this workload, full stop.
4. **Cost optimization is extreme and deliberate.** You're willing to trade operational effort for absolute minimum platform cost, and self-hosting genuinely saves you real money at your current scale after engineering time is honestly accounted for — not just on paper.
5. **Vendor independence is critical.** You need a vendor-agnostic cost-tracking layer that could theoretically be replaced or extended without a vendor relationship in the loop.

These are legitimate reasons, and none of them are excuses. LangWatch serves all five well.

## When to move to Uncoil

Conversely, move to Uncoil if:

1. **You run experiments regularly.** Weekly A/B tests or more frequent prompt iterations. Managed experiment workflows save weeks of engineering that would otherwise go into building and maintaining a homegrown eval pipeline.
2. **Quality matters as much as cost.** Your business decision-making relies on measuring accuracy, latency, or user satisfaction alongside cost, not cost alone — and you've been burned at least once by a cost optimization that quietly hurt quality.
3. **Operational simplicity is worth paying for.** You'd rather let Uncoil handle infrastructure than own that burden, and you've done the honest math on engineering time above.
4. **Your team is growing fast.** Adding 5–10 engineers per quarter means operational overhead grows non-linearly if it's tied to a self-hosted stack. Managed platforms scale without requiring you to hire dedicated SREs just to keep the observability layer up.
5. **You need compliance-grade audit trails for quality, not just cost.** Regulated workflows increasingly require evidence that outputs meet a quality bar, not just that spend was tracked — and that evidence needs to be generated automatically, not reconstructed after the fact.

## Protect the security boundary

Both tools handle sensitive data (LLM inputs and outputs, cost data, user behavior). Understand the boundaries before you commit to either.

**LangWatch (self-hosted):**
- Your infrastructure, your security model, end to end.
- Ensure your LangWatch instance is behind authentication and a VPN or private network — it is not secure by default just because it's self-hosted.
- Backup encryption is your responsibility, as is key rotation.
- Audit logs depend on your database and whatever logging you've layered on top.
- You control data retention and deletion, which is an advantage for teams with specific regulatory deletion requirements.
- Compliance evidence comes from your security posture, not the tool's certifications, because there aren't any to point to.

**Uncoil (managed):**
- Data encrypted in transit (TLS) and at rest.
- Role-based access control (RBAC) for team members, configurable per project.
- Audit logs built in — who accessed what, when, without additional instrumentation.
- SOC 2 Type II compliance on the roadmap.
- Data retention policies managed by Uncoil, with configurable retention windows per project.
- Compliance evidence includes Uncoil's certifications and audits, which can be handed directly to a customer's security review rather than assembled from scratch.

For regulated environments (healthcare, finance), discuss compliance posture with both vendors explicitly rather than assuming. LangWatch's self-hosted model may provide stronger guarantees in one specific sense — all data stays within your compliance boundary by construction. Uncoil's managed model includes compliance tooling and third-party audits but requires trust in the vendor's operational discipline.

The security trade-off is fundamental: LangWatch gives you control; Uncoil gives you professional management. Neither is inherently more secure — it depends on your team's security expertise, your compliance requirements, and, honestly, how disciplined your team actually is about patching a self-hosted service on schedule versus how disciplined a vendor whose entire business depends on staying secure will be.

## Making the operating decision

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Decision room" title="Choose based on your evaluation maturity and deployment constraints." body="If you need cost tracking only and can self-host, LangWatch wins on cost and control, especially at small scale. If you're iterating on models or running experiments, or if operational overhead is a growing concern, Uncoil's managed evaluation layer justifies the platform cost — often earlier than teams expect once engineering time is counted honestly. Many teams run both: LangWatch for historical audit, Uncoil for production workflows." tone="paper" /%}

The choice isn't binary. Some teams use LangWatch for cost audit and Uncoil for experiments, running both in parallel indefinitely. Others start with LangWatch and graduate to Uncoil once evaluation becomes a weekly workflow rather than an occasional one. The right answer depends on your team's size, your willingness to operate infrastructure, and whether evaluation is part of your roadmap now or later — and "later" has a way of arriving faster than teams plan for.

## Leave the reader with the next move

- [ ] Audit your current cost tracking gaps and operationalization burden, including engineering hours you haven't been counting.
- [ ] Define what "good enough evaluation" means for your primary use case (prompt experiments, model selection, feature rollout, user satisfaction).
- [ ] Estimate the operational cost of self-hosting LangWatch for your current scale and team size, using the worked numbers above as a starting template.
- [ ] If evaluation is required now, trial Uncoil for one sprint to understand the workflow before committing.
- [ ] If cost tracking alone is sufficient, deploy LangWatch and revisit this decision once trace volume or team size doubles.

The best time to add evaluation infrastructure is before you need it. The worst time is in the middle of a production incident, trying to explain to a customer why a model swap quietly degraded their results for two weeks before anyone noticed. Plan ahead.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Can I run LangWatch and Uncoil at the same time?" answer="Yes. Many teams send traces to both for cost audit and experimentation. The only trade-off is double instrumentation overhead (minimal) and duplicate storage. This is a valid hybrid strategy if you want cost redundancy and managed evaluation workflows, and it's common in regulated industries that want a self-hosted audit trail alongside a managed evaluation layer." /%}

{% faq-item question="If I start with LangWatch, can I migrate to Uncoil later without rewriting my code?" answer="Yes. Both platforms use similar SDK patterns. The migration typically requires swapping one SDK import and reconfiguring endpoints, not touching call sites throughout your codebase. You can run both in parallel for a week to verify data consistency before cutting over, which is the step most teams spend the bulk of their migration time on." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does LangWatch have any evaluation features at all?" answer="LangWatch focuses on observability and cost tracking. It doesn't have built-in eval workflows or experiment routing. You can integrate external evaluation tools or build your own scoring pipeline, but this requires custom code and ongoing maintenance. Uncoil has evaluation built into the platform, including scorer configuration, experiment routing, and annotation queues for human review." /%}

{% faq-item question="Is LangWatch truly free, or are there hidden costs?" answer="LangWatch is open-source and free to deploy. You pay for infrastructure (compute, storage, network) plus the engineering time to operate it. At small scales, this total is cheaper than Uncoil's per-trace pricing. At large scales, the operational overhead — specifically engineering hours for database and cluster management — often exceeds platform costs by a wide margin, as the worked numbers in this guide show." /%}

{% faq-item question="What if I need both self-hosting and evaluation capabilities?" answer="This is a genuinely challenging requirement. LangWatch is open-source but lacks evaluation features. Uncoil is managed-only. Your options are to build evaluation on top of LangWatch yourself, or compromise on one requirement — accept managed infrastructure for the evaluation layer while keeping LangWatch for cost audit, or build evaluation elsewhere. Open-source evaluation frameworks like DeepEval exist but require custom integration work and ongoing maintenance of their own." /%}
